        "eyebrow": "Why we exist",
        "heading": "Skincare built for the climate it is worn in",
        "heading_size": "h0",
        "body": "<p>Almost every skincare formula sold in India was developed somewhere with one climate and four mild seasons. India is not that country. A single year here runs through 45-degree heat, monsoon damp, ten hours a day of dry air conditioning, and particulate load that settles on skin by lunchtime.</p><p>Each of those attacks the barrier differently, and they overlap. Humidity stops sweat evaporating, so pores block. Air conditioning pulls water out for the rest of the working day. Hard water strips lipids during the wash itself, before a single product has been applied.</p><p>The usual answer is to sell the European formula unchanged and hope. The clearest example is vitamin C: L-ascorbic acid is the best-studied form and the least stable, and in an Indian bathroom in May it oxidizes into a brown liquid that does nothing. It was never a bad product. It was a product built for a different room.</p>",
